#use(std/code)

#section{Text Basics}::

Plain text is content; consecutive lines join into one paragraph and a blank
line starts a new one. The inline markup below comes from #code{std/base},
which is loaded by default — no #code{#use} needed

#code(lang: "plain")
	First paragraph, line one.
	First paragraph, line two.

	Second paragraph after a blank line.

	#para: An explicit paragraph, standing on its own.

	#emph{emphasis}, #strong{bold}, and #tt{inline code}.

	End a line early#linebreak and keep going in the same paragraph.

	Visit #link("https://example.com"){our homepage} for details.

Live:

First paragraph, line one.
First paragraph, line two

Second paragraph after a blank line

#para: An explicit paragraph, standing on its own

#emph{emphasis}, #strong{bold}, and #tt{inline code}

End a line early#linebreak and keep going in the same paragraph

Visit #link("https://example.com"){our homepage} for details

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		#code{#linebreak} is an inline hard line break — it stays inside the paragraph (unlike #code{#hr}, which is a
		block-level rule). HTML #tt{<br />}, CommonMark backslash break, Typst #code{#linebreak()}
	#item: Trailing spaces on a content line are literal output — they are preserved, not stripped
	#item: A whitespace-only line counts as blank (a paragraph break)
	#item
		#code{#link} takes the URL as a host-language string argument and the link text as the body; a non-string URL is an
		error
	#item: Markup nests freely — #code|{#link("…"){#emph{click #strong{here}}}}| is fine
